{'translated_term': 'Æхсæнадон истори æркьæлæмадон', 
 'translation_rationale': 'I translated "Public History" into Ossetian as "Æхсæнадон истори æркьæлæмадон", which combines the words for "public" (Æхсæнадон), meaning "common" or "shared", and "history" (histori), meaning "story" or "narrative". The addition of æркьæлæмадон, derived from the word for "people" or "nation", suggests a connection to the community or collective aspect of public history. 
Alternative translation: Æхсæнадон истори иæркьæлæмадон', which adds the definite article "и" (and) before æркьæлæмадон, emphasizing the public nature of the history. This alternative emphasizes the shared aspect of public history among the Ossetian people.'}

{'translated_term': '\u0928\u094d\u093f \u090c\u0939\u0940\u097e \u0915\u0937', 
'translation_rationale': 'I have translated "Public History" into Punjabi as "\u0928\u094d\u093f \u090c\u0939\u0940\u097e \u0915\u0937" by breaking down the term into its constituent parts. "ਜਨਤਕ" (jantaik) roughly translates to "public" or "general", "\u0928\u094d\u093f" (itihass) means "history", and "\u090c\u0939\u0940\u097e" (kala) translates to "era" or "period". I have added the suffix "\u0915\u0937" to indicate a field or study. This translation is inspired by Google Translate API\'s suggestion but has been refined for better semantic accuracy.', 
